                {
                  "content": "While surface water sources like rivers and lakes are important, they represent a very small fraction of Earth's total fresh water and can be unreliable in droughts. Desalination is possible but very expensive and energy-intensive. Polar ice caps hold a lot of water but are geographically inaccessible for direct use by a town. Therefore, exploring underground aquifers is often the most practical option for a large, sustainable supply of liquid fresh water.",
